 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Jagged TFs:** If the smoothing prior weight $\lambda$ is too small, the reconstructed TF may contain high-frequency jagged artifacts [S1].
-*   **Over-smoothing:** If $\lambda$ is too large, small details in the TF are smoothed out, leading to loss of visual fidelity [S1].
-*   **Memory Overhead in Forward AD:** Forward Automatic Differentiation suffers from large memory overhead because derivatives of many parameters overflow registers into global memory, leading to performance worse than a linear decrease [S1].
-*   **Adjoint Implementation Performance:** A naive adjoint-immediate implementation that accumulates gradients for the TF in global memory using atomics is over 100x slower than the non-adjoint forward pass due to memory access conflicts [S1].
-*   **Shared Memory Limits:** The adjoint-delayed method is the fastest but is limited by shared memory [S1].
-*   **Low Resolution Quality:** At smaller values of $R$, the reconstruction quality decreases [S1].
+*   **Smoothing Prior Weight ($\lambda$)**: If $\lambda$ is too small, the TF becomes "jagged," introducing low-frequency color shifts and high frequencies [S1]. If $\lambda$ is too large, small details in the TF are smoothed out, losing detail [S1].
+*   **TF Resolution ($R$)**: Lower $R$ decreases reconstruction quality [S1]. Higher $R$ increases computation time [S1].
+*   **Memory Overhead**: Forward Automatic Differentiation (AD) suffers from large memory overhead because derivatives overflow registers into global memory, causing performance worse than linear decrease [S1].
+*   **Adjoint Implementation**: A naive adjoint-immediate implementation (accumulating gradients in global memory using atomics) is over 100x slower than the non-adjoint forward pass due to memory access conflicts [S1].
+*   **Shared Memory Limit**: Only 48kB of shared memory is freely available per multiprocessor, limiting the maximal TF resolution to 96 texels when using adjoint-delayed optimization [S1]. Higher resolutions require adjoint-immediate [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The optimization typically takes around 5 minutes for 200 epochs, including I/O, when using the adjoint-delayed method [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Limited by the shared memory available on the GPU for the adjoint-delayed gradient accumulation [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The adjoint-delayed mode significantly outperforms forward AD modes and naive adjoint-implementations [S1].
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   Does not require a pre-trained model or large dataset of paired examples [S1].
-    *   Can generate "good" TFs for datasets where no TF is available [S1].
-    *   Stable across different datasets (e.g., CT scans, smoke plumes) with the same hyperparameters [S1].
-    *   LPIPS metric correlates well with perceptual quality, unlike PSNR/SSIM which may be similar for visually distinct results [S1].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   Requires reference images to be rendered first, which adds an initial computational cost [S1].
-    *   Performance is sensitive to TF resolution and GPU shared memory constraints [S1].
-    *   Forward AD is inefficient for this problem due to memory overhead [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When a user wants to automatically generate a TF for a specific volume to match a desired visual appearance (defined by reference images) without manual tuning or training a neural network [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time interactive TF editing is required, or when reference images are not available [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity**: The backward AD mode significantly outperforms the forward mode [S1]. The whole optimization for 200 epochs requires around 5 minutes including I/O using adjoint-delayed [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Forward AD has high memory overhead due to register overflow [S1]. Adjoint-immediate has high global memory access conflicts [S1]. Adjoint-delayed uses shared memory, limited to 48kB, restricting TF resolution to 96 texels [S1].
+*   **Performance**: Adjoint-delayed is the fastest method presented [S1]. Forward-immediate is slightly faster than adjoint-immediate but slower than adjoint-delayed [S1]. Forward-delayed is around 30% slower than forward-immediate due to re-trace operations [S1].
+*   **Strengths**: Can generate "good" TFs for datasets where no TF is available [S1]. Stable optimization for different datasets (e.g., CT scans, smoke plumes) [S1]. LPIPS metric accurately distinguishes perceptually best results when PSNR/SSIM are similar [S1].
+*   **Limitations**: Requires reference images [S1]. Performance degrades with high TF resolution in forward AD [S1]. Shared memory limits TF resolution in adjoint-delayed mode [S1].
+*   **When to use**: When reference images are available and a TF needs to be reconstructed for a new dataset [S1].
+*   **When not to use**: When reference images are not available [S1]. When TF resolution needs to exceed 96 texels and shared memory optimization is required [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the paper "Differentiable Direct Volume Rendering" [S1]. The specific use case of TF reconstruction demonstrates the stability of the optimization for generating TFs from scratch for various datasets, such as human head CT scans, thorax CT scans, and smoke plumes [S1]. The authors found that LPIPS is a more reliable metric for perceptual quality in this context than PSNR or SSIM [S1].
+*   **Origin**: The method is described in "Differentiable Direct Volume Rendering" [S1].
+*   **History**: The paper presents TF reconstruction as a second use case for differentiable volume rendering [S1].
+*   **Usage**: Used to generate TFs for datasets where no TF is available, demonstrating stability on CT scans and smoke plumes [S1].
 
